at (1,1) sends a horizontal corridor along its row until it reaches the column of the 3, then turns vertically down to the 3; the same 4 sends a vertical corridor down its column until the row of the 2, then turns horizontally to the 2. Pair 4 confirms the orientation isn't \"down/right\" but \"toward the target\": the 4 at (6,10) goes horizontally left to the 3's column then down, and vertically up to the 2's row then left. Pairs 2 and 3 fit the same rule, with the two corridors overlapping where they cross (which is why the fact sheet shows some diagonal-looking segments: they're just overlapping Ls). The coloured cells themselves are never overwritten.\n\nSo the rule is:\n- 4 → 3: horizontal first (along the 4's row to the 3's column), then vertical to the 3.\n- 4 → 2: vertical first (along the 4's column to the 2's row), then horizontal to the 2.\n\nFor the test input: 4 at (2,10), 3 at (11,7), 2 at (5,1). The 3-corridor runs left along row 2 from x=9 to x=7, then down column 7 from y=3 to y=10. The 2-corridor runs down column 10 from y=3 to y=5, then left along row 5 from x=9 to x=2.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    out = g.copy()\n    bg = background(g)\n\n    def find(c):\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == c)\n        return int(ys[0]), int(xs[0])\n\n    hy, hx = find(4)\n    ty3 = find(3)\n    ty2 = find(2)\n\n    def paint(y, x):\n        if out[y, x] == bg:\n            out[y, x] = 5\n\n    def hline(y, x0, x1):\n        for x in range(min(x0, x1), max(x0, x1) + 1):\n            paint(y, x)\n\n    def vline(x, y0, y1):\n        for y in range(min(y0, y1), max(y0, y1) + 1):\n            paint(y, x)\n\n    # Hub -> 3: horizontal leg first, then vertical leg.\n    y3, x3 = ty3\n    hline(hy, hx, x3)\n    vline(x3, hy, y3)\n    emit(out, \"L-shaped grey corridor from the 4 to the 3: along the 4's row to the 3's column, then down/up to the 3\")\n\n    # Hub -> 2: vertical leg first, then horizontal leg.\n    y2, x2 = ty2\n    vline(hx, hy, y2)\n    hline(y2, hx, x2)\n    emit(out, \"L-shaped grey corridor from the 4 to the 2: along the 4's column to the 2's row, then sideways to the 2\")\n\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Grey (5) corridors connect the hub to each destination with an L-shaped path, and the only question is which leg comes first."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Reading the evidence: in pair 1 the 4 at (1,1) sends a horizontal corridor along its row until it reaches the column of the 3, then turns vertically down to the 3; the same 4 sends a vertical corridor down its column until the row of the 2, then turns horizontally to the 2."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pairs 2 and 3 fit the same rule, with the two corridors overlapping where they cross (which is why the fact sheet shows some diagonal-looking segments: they're just overlapping Ls)."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Pairs 2 and 3 fit the same rule, with the two corridors overlapping where they cross (which is why the fact sheet shows some diagonal-looking segments: they're just overlapping Ls)."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"23a7571e7920f65c7b117502cb3bf19bc499ecde04822d00b2e26e43632f3fd6","task_sha256":"19ad9ba9739866aa7d8fd415f6ae397b88689ea2e645b02f1774fd42e3d59b52","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
